{"id":"snes-nso","name":"SNES Controller (Nintendo Switch Online)","vendor":"Nintendo","vid":"0x057E","pid":"0x2017","productString":"SNES Controller","manufacturerString":"Nintendo Co., Ltd.","type":"gamepad","connection":"bluetooth","bluetoothTransport":"classic","usbForm":"snes-nso-usb","versionNumber":0,"descriptor":"","inputReportSize":362,"notes":"The 170-byte Bluetooth HID descriptor every Switch Bluetooth pad serves, captured from SNES controllers for Nintendo Switch Online (darthcloud bt_traces sw_pair.log and steam_win10_sw_2x_pairing.pcapng, Nefarius.Utilities.Bluetooth Dumps). The pad's behavior is this profile's data, on switch-pro's mechanisms. Before a host speaks the protocol it sends the 12-byte 0x3F report with the D-pad as the hat and the four stick words at 0x8000, as the captures show, and B, A, Y, X, L, R, ZL, Select and Start as buttons 1 to 7, 9 and 10, and ZR as button 16 (BlueRetro sw.c, SDL_hidapi_switch.c HandleSimpleControllerState). No capture shows a button held. The protocol answers device type 0B (SDL_hidapi_nintendo.h, hid-nintendo.c, switchbrew), the byte after it 02 and the trailing 01 02 (NS-LIB-HID and HOJA, which a console accepts), and SPI reads from an image that is FF beside the device type, since the pad has no sticks and no motion sensor and no public dump of one exists. No public source gives its firmware version, and no host reads it. After the handshake the 0x30 report carries the Pro Controller's button bits with ZR on the ZR bit, the stick bytes zero, 80 in byte 2 and 00 in byte 12. The pad has no rumble. On the USB/IP backend the device presents snes-nso-usb under this profile's id and strings (usbForm).","layout":{"kind":"gamepad","source":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nintendo_Switch_Online","sticks":[],"triggers":[],"dpad":{"encoding":"hat","hatAxis":"Hat","hatPositions":8},"faceButtons":[{"role":"face_b","buttonIndex":0},{"role":"face_a","buttonIndex":1},{"role":"face_y","buttonIndex":2},{"role":"face_x","button